Task: multi-interrupt-tests + multi-interrupt-impl (plan agui_hitl_interrupts_a1f3c2d8, phase P2)

Why: spec FR3. ProcessEvent returned true on the first interrupt call,
so only one interrupt per event ever reached the client. Any later
proposal in the same event was unanswerable, and because the pending
records persisted server side did include it, a resume answering only
the interrupts the client had seen would fail validation with 'resume
missing interruptId'.

Changes:
- agui/interrupt_multi_test.go (new): three confirmation calls in one
  event yield exactly one RUN_FINISHED carrying three interrupts in part
  order, each with its own tool lifecycle emitted before the terminal
  event, and all three recorded in state.EmittedInterrupts. Plus a guard
  that the single-interrupt case still yields exactly one.
- agui/internal/stream/processor.go:
  - interruptCallHandler now returns (types.Interrupt, error) instead of
    finalizing the run itself.
  - EmitInterrupt renamed to buildToolCallInterrupt and reduced to
    building one interrupt plus its tool lifecycle.
  - New Processor.finishWithInterrupts owns snapshots, the single
    RunFinished, EmittedInterrupts and RunFinalized.
  - ProcessEvent accumulates pendingInterrupts across parts and finalizes
    after the parts loop.

Ordering decisions:
- finalizeLifecycle runs before the FIRST interrupt's tool lifecycle
  (preserving single-interrupt byte order) and again in
  finishWithInterrupts, which is idempotent, to catch anything a later
  part opened.
- Snapshots emit once per event, not once per interrupt. They describe
  session state and message history, so repeating them would be
  redundant and would change the single-interrupt wire.

Behaviour change worth noting: parts after an interrupt call are no
longer skipped, since the loop must keep scanning to find further
interrupt calls. In practice ADK emits interrupt calls as the whole
content, so no existing test observed the old skip.

Verification: TestConfirmationInterruptWireBaseline passes unchanged
(tool_call wire is byte identical, spec acceptance criterion 6). go fmt,
go vet, CI=true go test ./... all clean.
